# Environment Variables Reference

This file documents all environment variables needed for production deployment of EchoBrief AI.

## 🔐 Required (Production)

```bash
# Database
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://..."     # Railway Postgres connection string

# Redis
REDIS_URL="redis://..."             # Railway Redis connection string

# Authentication
AUTH_SECRET="..."                   # 32+ character secret for JWT signing

# AI Services
OPENAI_API_KEY="sk-..."            # OpenAI API key (GPT-5 + embeddings)
ASSEMBLYAI_API_KEY="..."           # AssemblyAI API key (transcription)

# Storage
R2_ACCOUNT_ID="..."                # Cloudflare R2 account ID
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID="..."             # Cloudflare R2 access key
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY="..."         # Cloudflare R2 secret key
R2_BUCKET="echobrief-audio"        # R2 bucket name
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL="https://..."   # R2 public URL for audio access

# Application
APP_URL="https://echobrief.ai"     # Public application URL
NODE_ENV="production"              # Environment mode
```

## 📊 Scalability Configuration (NEW)

```bash
# Database Connection Pooling
DB_POOL_SIZE=100                   # Default: 100 (was 10)
                                   # For 1M users: keep at 100, deploy 3-5 instances
                                   # Each instance gets 100 connections

# Worker Concurrency
WORKER_CONCURRENCY=10              # Default: 10 (was 1)
                                   # Processing worker: meetings/transcription jobs
                                   # For 1M users: keep at 10, deploy 10 worker instances
                                   # = 100 total parallel jobs

EXPORT_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=5        # Default: 5 (was 2)
                                   # Export worker: GDPR data exports
                                   # For 1M users: keep at 5, same 10 instances

# Redis Connection Pooling
REDIS_POOL_SIZE=10                 # Default: 10 (was 1)
                                   # Each API instance gets 10 Redis connections
                                   # Prevents single-connection bottleneck
```

## 🎨 Optional (Graceful Degradation)

```bash
# Email (without this, notifications disabled)
RESEND_API_KEY="re_..."            # Resend.com API key for transactional emails

# AI Model Selection
OPENAI_MODEL_PRIMARY="gpt-5"       # Default: gpt-5 (main model for analysis)
OPENAI_MODEL_LIGHT="gpt-5-mini"    # Default: gpt-5-mini (lightweight tasks)

# OAuth Integrations (V2 feature)
NOTION_CLIENT_ID="..."
NOTION_CLIENT_SECRET="..."
LINEAR_CLIENT_ID="..."
LINEAR_CLIENT_SECRET="..."
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID="..."
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET="..."

# Integration Token Encryption
INTEGRATION_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY="..."  # 44+ character base64 key for encrypting OAuth tokens

# Server
PORT=3000                          # Default: 3000
```

## 🚀 Railway Deployment Guide

### API Service (3-5 instances for 1M users)

```bash
# Required
DATABASE_URL=<from Railway Postgres addon>
REDIS_URL=<from Railway Redis addon>
AUTH_SECRET=<generate with: openssl rand -base64 32>
OPENAI_API_KEY=<from OpenAI dashboard>
ASSEMBLYAI_API_KEY=<from AssemblyAI dashboard>
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=<from Cloudflare R2>
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<from Cloudflare R2>
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<from Cloudflare R2>
R2_BUCKET=echobrief-audio
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://pub-xxxxx.r2.dev
APP_URL=https://echobrief.ai
NODE_ENV=production

# Scalability (NEW)
DB_POOL_SIZE=100
REDIS_POOL_SIZE=10

# Optional
RESEND_API_KEY=<from Resend dashboard>
PORT=3000
```

### Worker Service (10 instances for 1M users)

```bash
# Required (same as API)
DATABASE_URL=<from Railway Postgres addon>
REDIS_URL=<from Railway Redis addon>
OPENAI_API_KEY=<from OpenAI dashboard>
ASSEMBLYAI_API_KEY=<from AssemblyAI dashboard>
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=<from Cloudflare R2>
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<from Cloudflare R2>
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<from Cloudflare R2>
R2_BUCKET=echobrief-audio
NODE_ENV=production

# Scalability (NEW)
DB_POOL_SIZE=100                   # Workers also need DB access
REDIS_POOL_SIZE=10                 # Workers use Redis for queue
WORKER_CONCURRENCY=10              # 10 parallel jobs per instance
EXPORT_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=5        # 5 parallel exports per instance

# Optional
RESEND_API_KEY=<from Resend dashboard>
```

## 📈 Scaling Recommendations

### For 10K users (1 instance each)
```bash
DB_POOL_SIZE=100
WORKER_CONCURRENCY=10
EXPORT_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=5
REDIS_POOL_SIZE=10
```

### For 100K users (3-5 API, 5 workers)
```bash
# Same settings, just more instances
DB_POOL_SIZE=100      # × 5 instances = 500 total connections
WORKER_CONCURRENCY=10 # × 5 instances = 50 parallel jobs
```

### For 1M users (5 API, 10 workers)
```bash
# Same settings + PgBouncer
DB_POOL_SIZE=100      # × 5 API = 500
                      # × 10 workers = 1000
                      # Use PgBouncer to pool 1000 → 300 real connections

WORKER_CONCURRENCY=10 # × 10 instances = 100 parallel jobs
                      # = 28,800 meetings/day capacity ✅
```

## 🔍 Verification Commands

### Check current configuration
```bash
# SSH into Railway container
railway run bash

# Check environment variables
echo $DB_POOL_SIZE
echo $WORKER_CONCURRENCY
echo $REDIS_POOL_SIZE

# Test database connection
node -e "require('./dist/server/db').getSql().raw('SELECT 1')"

# Test Redis connection
node -e "require('./dist/server/services/redis').getRedis().ping().then(console.log)"
```

### Monitor connection usage
```bash
# Database connections (from Railway Postgres)
psql $DATABASE_URL -c "SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE datname = 'railway';"

# Redis connections (from Railway Redis)
redis-cli INFO clients | grep connected_clients

# Worker throughput (from Railway logs)
railway logs --filter="job.*complete" --tail=100
```

## ⚠️ Important Notes

1. **AUTH_SECRET**: NEVER commit this to git. Generate with `openssl rand -base64 32`
2. **R2 Keys**: Keep these secret. Rotate annually or if compromised
3. **API Keys**: Monitor usage/costs in provider dashboards
4. **DB_POOL_SIZE**: Don't exceed your database's max_connections limit (Railway Postgres = 300)
5. **WORKER_CONCURRENCY**: Start with 10, monitor memory usage, adjust if needed

## 🔄 Secret Rotation

### Rotate AUTH_SECRET (every 6 months)
```bash
1. Generate new secret: openssl rand -base64 32
2. Add as AUTH_SECRET_NEW in Railway
3. Update auth middleware to accept BOTH keys
4. Wait 30 days (JWT expiry)
5. Swap: AUTH_SECRET_NEW → AUTH_SECRET
6. Remove AUTH_SECRET_NEW
```

### Rotate R2 Keys (annually)
```bash
1. Create new access key in Cloudflare R2 dashboard
2. Update Railway env vars
3. Delete old key from Cloudflare
```

### Rotate API Keys (on policy change)
```bash
1. Generate new key in provider dashboard
2. Update Railway env var
3. Revoke old key
```

## 📊 Cost Implications

| Variable | Impact on Cost | Notes |
|----------|----------------|-------|
| DB_POOL_SIZE | None | Same Postgres tier |
| WORKER_CONCURRENCY | High | More workers = more instances = higher cost |
| REDIS_POOL_SIZE | None | Same Redis tier |
| OPENAI_MODEL_PRIMARY | Very High | gpt-5 = $5/M tokens, gpt-5-mini = $0.5/M |
| ASSEMBLYAI_API_KEY | Very High | $0.10/hour transcription |

**Tip:** Monitor AI costs daily. They scale linearly with usage and can explode if misconfigured.
